WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.300
In fact, the students should be
encouraged to search for whatever they're

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.740
interested in and the teacher should
help that person find that information.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.330
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:17.900
Can critical thinking be taught, and how
does that relate to language learning?

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.210
So remember, by the way you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.160
click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.030
Those of you who follow my videos
know that I'm a great believer

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.560
in listening and reading.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.790
I believe that the language has
to come into us from outside.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.560
We have to listen and read and acquire
words before we can start speaking.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.129
Uh, at some point we
have to start speaking.

00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:46.470
At some point, we have to speak a lot.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.839
Uh, when we do start speaking, obviously
we want to work on improving, uh,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.879
our ability to speak,our accuracy and
the use of words, our pronunciation,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.959
and so forth, but we first have
to get the language into us.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:09.509
Well, I think much the same is true when
it comes to our ability to reason, okay.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.259
And I have been on, what's
known as list serves.

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:18.060
In other words, email communities
with teachers, and very often teachers

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.100
are very interested in the subject
of teaching critical thinking or

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.950
what they call higher order thinking.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:33.270
I have always been very suspicious of the
ability of the average teacher to teach

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.240
critical thinking skills or higher order.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.600
If in fact, such a thing
exists, thinking skills.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:44.400
The assumption there is that their
students have a lower order thinking,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.440
these are teachers of English.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.990
Uh, and so rather than focusing in on
how do I get my students to read more?

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.089
How do I get my students to listen more?

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.340
Uh, how do I provide them with content
of interest, things that are relevant to

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.910
them, things that they're familiar with,
that they're going to want to engage with?

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.410
How do I stimulate them?

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:10.229
How do I create that, that thirst for
reading and listening rather there is

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:16.320
this sort of what I call a myth that
they can teach a set of skills that will

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.795
enable their, in this case these were
English teachers teaching, uh, you know,

00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:27.795
English as a second language that these
foreigners might be, um, immigrant from

00:02:28.335 --> 00:02:32.955
Iran or from Poland or from Nicaragua
that somehow because they didn't speak

00:02:32.955 --> 00:02:39.255
English well, somehow their, uh, critical
thinking skills weren't, uh, as developed

00:02:39.255 --> 00:02:48.119
as, as, as the skills of the teacher, I
have always felt that the most important

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:55.739
sort of prerequisite for critical thinking
is knowledge of the subject matter.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.839
You cannot judge because part of this
whole critical thinking is that, uh,

00:03:00.899 --> 00:03:07.200
people need to be better equipped to,
uh, you know, uh, judge, uh, what's

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.899
true or not true in the media and, and
so forth, which is all fine and dandy.

00:03:12.899 --> 00:03:19.125
We should be, uh, a little, uh, skeptical
about what we read and, and we should

00:03:19.125 --> 00:03:22.215
be trying to read through and see what
we agree and what we don't agree with.

00:03:22.965 --> 00:03:30.000
However, in most cases, the positions
that we hold are based on our emotions,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.530
we jump to a conclusion and then
we try to justify that conclusion.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.100
I do that.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:35.850
Everyone does that.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.320
If we didn't all do that, we'd all
arrive at the same conclusion because

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.245
we've been follow some irrational
method of critical thinking and, you

00:03:45.245 --> 00:03:49.565
know, objectively evaluate all the
information and we'd arrive at the

00:03:49.565 --> 00:03:54.905
only correct, uh, conclusion, but
there is no only correct conclusion.

00:03:54.905 --> 00:03:59.915
There's lots of different conclusions, but
generally speaking, the more knowledgeable

00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:05.385
a person is about a particular subject
the more I value their opinion.

00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:11.535
I don't think you can, uh, somehow
skip the stage of just as in language

00:04:11.565 --> 00:04:14.265
learning, acquiring words and phrases.

00:04:14.265 --> 00:04:17.775
You can't speak before you have
a lot of words and phrases in you

00:04:17.775 --> 00:04:19.365
until you have good comprehension.

00:04:19.454 --> 00:04:20.715
You're not going to speak well.

00:04:20.954 --> 00:04:25.635
Well, until you have read a lot
on a subject or experienced that

00:04:25.755 --> 00:04:30.255
situation, acquired a lot of
information and knowledge and

00:04:30.255 --> 00:04:34.680
experience about that subject, your
opinion is perhaps not that important.

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:40.229
And there's no amount of theoretical,
you know, critical thinking skills

00:04:40.469 --> 00:04:45.330
that are going to substitute for
solid knowledge of the subject matter.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.320
And the way to get knowledge
of the subject matter is to

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.599
read a lot, to read well.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:57.510
And so rather than, with these ESL
teachers trying to teach critical

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:04.200
thinking, if you encourage those learners
to read a lot, to read widely initially,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.260
to read on subjects of interest to
them, they will find different sources

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.099
of information because the chances are
the teacher who's teaching critical

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:18.120
thinking wants to sort of direct the
learner in a certain way direction

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.660
so that the learner will come to
the same conclusion as the teacher.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.770
And so that this whole teaching of
critical thinking in effect becomes a way

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.990
of controlling what the learner thinks.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:32.280
And I think there's already
too much control by teachers.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.789
In other words, this is the
book you're supposed to read.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.739
This is the newspaper you should read.

00:05:37.739 --> 00:05:40.590
This is a subject matter you
should read is sort of the

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.119
teacher directing the student.

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:45.539
In fact, the students should be
encouraged to search for whatever

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:49.590
they're interested in and the teacher
should help that person find that

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.390
information just as I'm doing with Sahra.

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:53.580
I tell her what I'm interested in.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.690
I'm interested in history.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.680
So she gives me this wonderful
series on persian history.

00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:05.340
Um, this again stimulates me to not only
read on in Persian, which I'm limited

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.680
in my ability to do, but also to look
up books in English on Iranian history.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:16.620
And so I'm constantly, through reading
I'm acquiring more information, not with

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.090
a goal that I'm going to think critically,
but obviously if I read a book on

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.909
iranian history and then I read a book on
Turkish history and, and then the Arabic

00:06:26.909 --> 00:06:28.560
history and written by different people.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.400
Or when I was doing a Ukrainian and
Polish and Russian, then I would

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.510
listen to a, you know, a Russian
version of Ukrainian history, a

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.820
Polish version or Ukrainian version.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.390
And out of all of these different
bits of info, based some extent on my,

00:06:45.465 --> 00:06:50.915
you know, um, conclusions I arrived
at because of say a, a degree of

00:06:50.915 --> 00:06:53.315
sympathy for the Ukrainians, underdogs.

00:06:53.715 --> 00:06:56.985
Uh, but someone else who is more
sympathetic to the Poles or to

00:06:56.985 --> 00:07:00.315
the Russians might find that
the Polish or Russian version

00:07:00.345 --> 00:07:02.925
is, is closer to the truth.

00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:07.455
Uh, but I don't think it's any particular
critical thinking skill that's going

00:07:07.455 --> 00:07:08.895
to take us to those conclusions.

00:07:09.075 --> 00:07:11.145
It's more a mindset that we have.

00:07:11.355 --> 00:07:15.735
We've arrived at for whatever emotional,
uh, whatever emotion-based reason.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.840
Of course, we use reason to
try and justify our position.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:23.950
And hopefully we're open-minded
enough to listen to other people

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:29.200
present, you know, the justification
for their position using reason.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.940
But ultimately I think, I mean, it's
very difficult to persuade people, uh, to

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.380
give up a point of view that they have.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.340
That's been my experience.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.130
Uh, it can be interesting.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.390
And if we're open-minded enough to
accept the, the fact that people can have

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:46.680
different points of view and that doesn't
necessarily make them a bad person.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.360
That doesn't mean that I have
to agree with them because

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.620
I asked for their opinion.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.409
Uh, they don't have to agree with me.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:59.789
We can just share our views and continue
to disagree and that's fine and dandy.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:07.260
But, uh, I say this because so often I
think that teachers are prone to pursue

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:11.340
certain fads, like teaching critical
thinking, higher order thinking or

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:15.539
this learning styles myth, which has
been largely debunked by which a whole

00:08:15.539 --> 00:08:19.380
bunch of teachers pursued that the,
you know, are you a kinetic learner?

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:20.700
Are you a visual learner?

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:25.515
And of course, Uh, cognitive
scientists, uh, assure us and, uh,

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:26.985
all kinds of tests have demonstrated.

00:08:27.025 --> 00:08:30.825
This is not the case that the
learning styles is, is a bit

00:08:30.825 --> 00:08:33.135
of a, a bit of a red herring.

00:08:33.525 --> 00:08:36.945
So I just thought, I'd mentioned
that my view on this whole subject

00:08:36.945 --> 00:08:41.805
of critical thinking, uh, it's
possible that there are people who

00:08:41.805 --> 00:08:43.095
are expert at critical thinking.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:49.440
Who are, you know, methodical and
follow the scientific method and, uh,

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:54.420
pursue a variety of different sources
of information and then apply very, uh,

00:08:54.420 --> 00:09:00.180
you know, consistent, uh, uh, rational
methods for arriving at their conclusions.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.210
I still suspect they're going to be,
you know, it'll be flavored by their,

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.140
uh, prejudice going, preconceived
idea going in, but that people who

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.489
can do that is a small minority.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.280
The vast majority of people
don't have those skills.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.010
And therefore the vast majority
of teachers are not in a

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:17.719
position to teach those skills.

00:09:17.869 --> 00:09:23.300
And I don't really think those
skills exist outside of what we

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:27.229
call domain knowledge, knowledge
about the subject matter and to get

00:09:27.229 --> 00:09:31.189
that good knowledge of the subject
matter, you have to read a lot.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.740
So the focus should be on
getting students to read more.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.570
Anyway, that's just a bit of a rant there
off my normal subject yet related because

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:44.280
as with language learning it's input
that helps us grow whether in language

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.320
or in discovering more about the world.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.330
Thank you for listening.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:49.350
Bye for now.

